      "writtenOn": "2022-12-08T18:20:36Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "My main reason against this approach is that it breaks the \"API definition is Rust traits definition\" principle. The original idea is that all API is self-documented by the trait definitions, and these API definition can be projected to any IPC not necessarily D-Bus. For example, `SetL2capMode` is projected to `org.chromium.BluetoothQA` without being defined in `IBluetoothQA`.\n\nOption 3 would be my preferred way if unifying to a single QA interface is a must. But I agree with your doc that it requires some non-trivial refactor. Therefore my overall suggestion this time is to use option 4 and make those QA methods obvious, i.e. by grouping the definitions close to each other, commenting them, and maybe even naming them with `QA*`. Optionally, to address the security/privacy concern in the short term you may protect this methods with a run-time flag like `--enable-qa` which is much easier to implement than option 3 without breaking the API principle like option 2.",
      "parentUuid": "4be4fbb6_306569f5",
      "revId": "882cd5947d200b7a4ba675b5a310db2bd2b8e3a7",
